abstract: "One Poincaré inequality on the source measure makes every full iteration of the exact continuous Sinkhorn algorithm shrink the entropic dual gap by at most C_P L^2/(C_P L^2 + lambda^2), from the first iteration, for every bounded initial potential and every target (Theorem 2), so about (C_P L^2/lambda^2) log(1/eps) iterations suffice. This covers quadratic or bilinear cost with Gaussian or exponential-tailed sources and compactly supported targets such as point clouds (Theorem 3, Corollary 4), and turns the curvature estimate of Chizat, Delalande and Vaskevicius into a global factor C/(C + lambda) in their bounded-density settings (Corollary 5). The exponent 2 is sharp in general but no single fixed source attains order lambda^2 (Theorem 6, Propositions 7-8, Corollary 9). Chiarini, Conforti, Greco and Tamanini obtained the same rate shape under a Talagrand inequality and Hessian bounds. Population measures only; unrefereed candidate; internal AI-assisted review only; scripts are floating-point checks. Text and figures CC0-1.0; code MIT."
